WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.470
NO ONE IS IMMUNE FROM THE RAVAGES OF TIME.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.880
THE DETRIMENTAL EFFECTS OF AGING ARE INEVITABLE,
RIGHT?

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:19.510
BUT WHAT IF WE COULD TURN BACK THE CLOCK AND
REPLACE WORN OUT OR DAMAGED BODY PARTS WITH

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:21.610
COMPLETELY NEW ONES?

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:26.410
IT MAY SOUND LIKE SCIENCE FICTION, BUT RESEARCHERS
STUDYING CELL GROWTH ARE CURRENTLY WORKING

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:28.080
TO MAKE IT SCIENCE FACT.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:33.230
IMAGINE A FUTURE WHERE NO ONE WAITS ON AN
ORGAN TRANSPLANT LIST.

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:38.540
IF YOUR KIDNEY FAILED, YOU COULD HAVE A REPLACEMENT
MADE-TO ORDER IN A LAB.

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:43.160
USING YOUR OWN CELLS, TECHNICIANS COULD BUILD
A NEW KIDNEY USING A 3D PRINTER.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:47.210
AND WITH THIS PRINTED ORGAN, YOU’D QUICKLY
GET BACK TO A PRODUCTIVE LIFE.

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:52.510
IMAGINE A FUTURE WHERE IF YOU’RE DISFIGURED
IN AN UNFORTUNATE ACCIDENT, A PILL COULD CHANGE

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:58.449
THE ELECTRICAL CHARGE AT THE SITE OF THE INJURY
CAUSING YOUR TOES TO REGENERATE.

00:00:58.449 --> 00:01:03.480
IN THE FUTURE, IF YOU SUFFER FROM HEART FAILURE,
A NEW ORGAN COULD BE GROWN ON A CADAVER HEART

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:06.360
SCAFFOLD USING YOUR OWN STEM CELLS.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:11.430
AND WITH THIS NEW HEART, YOU COULD GET BACK
TO RUNNING.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:16.590
WHILE THESE THREE FUTURISTIC TREATMENTS ARE
ONLY IN THE EXPERIMENTAL STAGE, THERE IS ONE

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:22.680
SCIENCE-FICTION SOUNDING THERAPY THAT IS ALREADY
BEING USED ON HUMANS.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:24.530
TAKE THE CASE OF MARINE SGT.

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:28.370
BRIAN SMITH WHO WAS INJURED WHILE LEADING
HIS SQUAD DOWN ONE OF THE MOST TREACHEROUS

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:30.890
ROADS IN FALLUJAH, IRAQ.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:38.010
I was on a mission to get my squad familiar
to the new area of operation that we were

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:39.010
in.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:41.400
That entailed traveling down IED Alley.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:46.979
SMITH’S HUMVEE STRUCK AN I-E-D OR IMPROVISED
EXPLOSIVE DEVICE.

00:01:46.979 --> 00:01:55.590
We were just traveling and all of a sudden
we got hit, and I believe I went out for a

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:56.590
second.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:02.170
A PIECE OF SHRAPNEL SLICED THROUGH THE TRICEP
AND BICEP OF HIS RIGHT ARM, SEVERING A MAJOR

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:05.600
ARTERY AND LEAVING HIS MUSCLES IN TATTERS.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.549
I realized I couldn’t move my right arm.

00:02:07.549 --> 00:02:11.640
At first I thought it was gone, but I could
feel that it was still there, and I could

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.440
feel the blood—my blood from my artery pumping
into my hand as I reached over.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.760
SGT SMITH WAS AIRLIFTED TO GERMANY FOR TREATMENT.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:24.620
BACK IN THE U.S,, REHABILITATION OF HIS ARM
LEFT HIM WEAK, UNABLE TO ASSIST WITH THE CARE

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:26.000
OF HIS 9 MONTH OLD SON.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:27.660
He was go, go, go, go.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:31.670
One of the issues is trying to care for him.

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:34.530
I couldn’t pick him up if he was squirming
because I only had one hand.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:38.840
I couldn’t change him because you want one
hand to try to keep the kid still.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:42.910
BRIAN COMPENSATED FOR THE WEAKNESS BY USING
THE STRENGTH IN HIS BACK.

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:46.840
TWO YEARS LATER HE WAS ABLE TO JOIN THE BANGOR
POLICE FORCE.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.540
BUT THAT WEAKNESS IN HIS ARM WAS ALWAYS ON
HIS MIND.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:52.940
My concerns about my arm was anything that
required pulling.

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:56.840
You’re trying to take somebody into custody,
sometimes you have to pry their arm out.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:01.230
I would have to do it with my right hand but
I knew I was now significantly weaker in that

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:02.920
hand versus my left hand.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:07.120
BRIAN HEARD ABOUT AN EXPERIMENTAL PROCEDURE
AT THE UNIVERSITY OF PITTSBURGH THAT USES

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:14.790
AN EXTRACELLULAR MATRIX TO REGROW DAMAGED
MUSCLE TISSUE.A YEAR AFTER TREATMENT IN 2013,

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:20.950
THINGS WERE REMARKABLY DIFFERENT WITH HIS
ARM.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:29.840
The big thing is I was able to hold my child
in my right arm longer than I ever have.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:33.490
BRIAN HAD LOST ONE-THIRD OF HIS BICEP.

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:34.630
WITH E.C.M.

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:38.470
HE WAS ABLE TO REGROW ALMOST ALL OF THE MISSING
MUSCLE TISSUE.

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:40.090
Today my arm feels great.

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:44.310
My endurance is there, that was the big thing
for me.

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:51.380
NOW BRIAN CAN DO HIS JOB WITH COMPLETE CONFIDENCE
AND BE THE HANDS-ON FATHER HE WANTED TO BE.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:52.790
Good evening.

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:55.610
Welcome to our program.

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:58.430
Thank you for coming.

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:03.640
Being forever young the future and the promise
of human regeneration.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:08.610
So without further ado, let’s meet our scientist
beginning with the director of regenerative

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:14.490
medicine research and director center for
cell and organ biotechnology at the Texas

00:04:14.490 --> 00:04:16.450
Heart Institute in Houston.

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:20.010
She also holds faculty appointments at Texas
A&amp;M and Rice University.

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:26.430
Please welcome Doris Taylor.

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:30.509
Our next guest is a professor in the department
of surgery and the deputy director of the

00:04:30.509 --> 00:04:34.289
McGowan Institute for regenerative medicine
at the University of Pittsburgh.

00:04:34.289 --> 00:04:40.409
Dr. Badylak is a past president of the Tissue
Engineering Regenerative Medicine International

00:04:40.409 --> 00:04:41.409
Society.

00:04:41.409 --> 00:04:47.479
Please welcome Stephen Badylak.

00:04:47.479 --> 00:04:52.939
Also joining us is the principal investigator
in the Tufts Center for Regenerative and Developmental

00:04:52.939 --> 00:04:57.669
Biology and research professor in the department
of Biology at Tufts University.

00:04:57.669 --> 00:05:03.960
She is also faculty in the EBICS Program at
MIT and a member of the Allen Discovery Center

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:04.960
at Tufts.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:11.729
Please welcome Dany Spencer Adams.

00:05:11.729 --> 00:05:16.919
Our final guest is an associate professor
and associate director of the Nancy E. and

00:05:16.919 --> 00:05:23.039
Peter C. Meinig School of Biomedical Engineering
at Cornell University in beautiful Ithaca.

00:05:23.039 --> 00:05:27.620
He has been recognized with awards given by
the Society for Heart Valve Disease and the

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:29.520
Biomedical Engineering Society.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:32.460
Please welcome Jonathan Butcher.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:34.350
Welcome.

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:40.639
So we want to start tonight’s conversation
by asking each of you, beginning with Dany,

00:05:40.639 --> 00:05:46.789
just to give a little bit of a primer if you
will of just exactly what you are working

00:05:46.789 --> 00:05:47.789
on.

00:05:47.789 --> 00:05:51.719
Well actually I came to study regeneration
through studying generation.

00:05:51.719 --> 00:05:56.999
And I believe you’re about to see a movie
I made showing the generation of a face in

00:05:56.999 --> 00:05:58.139
a frog embryo.

00:05:58.139 --> 00:06:00.770
So there you’re looking at a frog embryo
right now.

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:05.169
And that bright spot coming up over the horizon
that is actually the central nervous system

00:06:05.169 --> 00:06:10.490
forming and you’re going to see spots that
are making where the face is going to be.

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:16.639
And what those spots are is a dye that is
showing us where the cells are more negative

00:06:16.639 --> 00:06:18.330
on the inside than they are on the outside.

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:21.409
So it’s an electrical characteristic of
the cells.

00:06:21.409 --> 00:06:29.969
And what we’ve found by studying these development
processes is these electrical signals help

00:06:29.969 --> 00:06:35.139
the embryo know where to put things and very
importantly, how to shape things.

00:06:35.139 --> 00:06:39.749
And that turns out to be a very important
aspect of regeneration which is how do you

00:06:39.749 --> 00:06:42.719
get the proper shape for function.

00:06:42.719 --> 00:06:44.270
And shape turns out to be important.

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:45.680
We’re going to come back to that.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:50.729
Stephen, how did you actually come to grow
muscle tissue?

00:06:50.729 --> 00:06:52.380
Tell us about that.

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:58.600
Our approach is a little different to regenerative
medicine as you saw in Sargent Smith there.

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:05.539
Our intent is to provide the environment that
the body normally uses to send signals to

00:07:05.539 --> 00:07:11.050
say ok stem cells come on over here and form
this particular type of tissue or these cells

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:12.050
should proliferate.

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:16.110
In other words, it’s sort of like the movie
“build it and they will come.”

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:22.389
What we want to do is provide the micro-environment
called the extracellular matrix and then let

00:07:22.389 --> 00:07:24.389
the body do the rest of the work.

00:07:24.389 --> 00:07:29.699
So it’s a little different approach but
it seems to be working.

00:07:29.699 --> 00:07:31.849
Doris, you have a different approach altogether.

00:07:31.849 --> 00:07:32.900
Tell us about that.

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:38.219
Well, heart disease is the number one killer
of men, women and children so what we’ve

00:07:38.219 --> 00:07:43.409
done for the last 20 years is work on ways
to repair the damage to the heart.

00:07:43.409 --> 00:07:45.169
And we started with genes.

00:07:45.169 --> 00:07:49.300
But what we realized is that you have to get
lucky and pick the right genes.

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:54.249
And that cells were little gene factories
so we moved to cells.

00:07:54.249 --> 00:08:00.550
But if you going to put a cell in the heart
just like Steve said, it needs the right environment.

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:07.400
And so it’s probably true that we’re never
going to get enough cells to take a fully

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:13.850
damaged, leathery skinned scar and make it
into a healthy heart again.

00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:18.749
So what we said is, wow we need an environment.

00:08:18.749 --> 00:08:20.589
Let’s take a heart.

00:08:20.589 --> 00:08:23.589
Let’s give nature the tools and get out
of the way.

00:08:23.589 --> 00:08:29.039
So today I work on building a heart in the
lab by taking stem cells and putting them

00:08:29.039 --> 00:08:30.699
in what we call a ghost heart.

00:08:30.699 --> 00:08:36.750
A heart where we have already stripped all
of the cells to let nature provide those cues

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:38.720
to rebuild the heart.

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:40.010
Amazing, amazing.

00:08:40.010 --> 00:08:45.960
Jonathan, even more amazing or equally as
amazing is printing a heart valve.

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:51.160
How does one begin to even thinking about
printing a heart valve?

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:58.510
So I think I came into this approach as a
traditional mechanical engineer.

00:08:58.510 --> 00:09:03.870
Like Dany, I heard a lecture on developmental
biology of the heart.

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:12.430
And this biologist proceeded to describe how
the heart forms inside of a week in this animal

00:09:12.430 --> 00:09:17.510
and here I am trying to engineer something
to change the protein in months.

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:21.861
And so clearly there was so much natural engineering
that was going on that we didn’t understand

00:09:21.861 --> 00:09:29.750
and so I shifted training in to developmental
biology and now in my current lab, we combine

00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:35.840
both of those sets of tools to be able to
try to bio-fabricate these organs.

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:42.310
Well let’s get to the idea of how you are
actually doing this and what this stuff actually

00:09:42.310 --> 00:09:43.310
looks like.

00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:47.790
So Doris take us through the how to make a
heart, if you will.

00:09:47.790 --> 00:09:51.130
Well-
and you can do this at home as I understand

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:52.130
it.

00:09:52.130 --> 00:09:53.130
It’s available online?

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:54.420
that’s right, that’s right.

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:57.620
There are blogs about how to do this online.

00:09:57.620 --> 00:10:04.340
And I think that illustrates an amazing point
that people really want to understand the

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:08.370
science of this and the biology of this and
there’s such an unmet need.

00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:14.260
I had a high school kid call me last week
and say our family’s barbecuing a pig in

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:20.180
the yard and my family gave me the heart and
it’s in the refrigerator.

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:24.070
What do I do?

00:10:24.070 --> 00:10:25.070
I told her.

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:26.170
So what do you do?

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:32.150
Well, if you’re going to build a heart,
you need cells, but beating cells right here

00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:34.190
in a dish are not enough to make a heart.

00:10:34.190 --> 00:10:36.210
You need a place to put those cells.

00:10:36.210 --> 00:10:39.460
You need a framework or a scaffold.

00:10:39.460 --> 00:10:46.580
So what we did is we took a heart, a pig heart
or a human heart, we now work routinely with

00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:47.580
human hearts.

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:49.710
And we wash the cells out.

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:57.400
We use baby shampoo, literally, to wash the
cells out of the heart and you can see here

00:10:57.400 --> 00:11:02.460
on the video, a pig’s heart in this case,
with no cells.

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:04.470
Is that collagen?

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:06.010
What is it?

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:09.090
It’s the extracellular matrix, proteins.

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:14.590
If you touch it, it feels a little like a
jello wiggler.

00:11:14.590 --> 00:11:20.290
And for those of you who aren’t old enough
to know what a jello wiggler is, it’s dehydrated

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:21.290
jello.

00:11:21.290 --> 00:11:22.690
It’s pretty tough.

00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:27.910
But we can then put cells back into that heart.

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:28.910
OK

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:34.530
And when we put cells back in what’s so
amazing is you put a blood vessel cell in

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:35.980
and it goes to the blood vessel.

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:39.740
You put a heart muscle cell in and it goes
to the muscle.

00:11:39.740 --> 00:11:41.290
We call it our smart heart.

00:11:41.290 --> 00:11:43.010
It knows what to do.

00:11:43.010 --> 00:11:49.080
Here you see a heart where we put cells back
in and it’s pink now because it’s beginning

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:56.350
to be more muscle and it’s moving because
you can’t just put a stem cell in a heart

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:57.670
and let it sit there.

00:11:57.670 --> 00:12:01.860
It has to act like a heart, look like a heart,
and work like a heart.

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:03.900
For the cells to know what to do.

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:05.840
To grow up and become a heart.

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:08.360
So it has to have a blood pressure.

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:11.670
It has to have lungs and oxygen.

00:12:11.670 --> 00:12:15.560
This is a human heart in one of our bioreactors.

00:12:15.560 --> 00:12:22.130
So a bioreactor is basically an artificial
body.

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:23.940
It’s got blood.

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:25.340
It’s got oxygen.

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:28.660
It’s got temperature.

00:12:28.660 --> 00:12:31.860
And it’s got an immune system.

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:36.870
You see here the plastic container, that’s
the bioreactor.

00:12:36.870 --> 00:12:37.990
Keeps it sterile.

00:12:37.990 --> 00:12:38.990
Keeps it warm.

00:12:38.990 --> 00:12:42.080
Food, clothing, shelter, everything that you
need to build a heart.

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:49.090
So we put stem cells back in and they mature
over time if we give them the right cues.

00:12:49.090 --> 00:12:50.770
It’s pretty remarkable.

00:12:50.770 --> 00:12:56.650
And you can do it with anything, heart, liver,
kidney, lung, pancreas.

00:12:56.650 --> 00:12:57.650
Unbelievable.

00:12:57.650 --> 00:12:58.800
Stephen, tell us your story.

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:03.340
How are you doing- what are you doing to get
muscle?

00:13:03.340 --> 00:13:05.540
What we do is start with the matrix.

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:08.080
Again with the matrix.

00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:11.360
Back to the matrix, that’s right.

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:16.930
You can isolate every tissue in the body of
every person in this audience has extracellular

00:13:16.930 --> 00:13:18.420
matrix around the cells.

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:20.600
That’s the scaffolding material.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:24.270
And in this video what you see is, that’s
Scott in my lab.

00:13:24.270 --> 00:13:26.880
That’s a pig bladder.

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:35.010
You go to the slaughterhouse and pick up and
instead of them being a byproduct of the agriculture

00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:39.040
industry, it would have been fertilizer or
food or something else if we didn’t pick

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:40.040
them up.

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:43.290
And we take away the muscle layers and we’re
left with only the layers that we want.

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:47.310
And so that’s that thin layer that Scott
was holding up there.

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:53.150
And then we take out all of the cells and
what you’re left with is this friendly,

00:13:53.150 --> 00:13:55.210
inductive material.

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:58.290
In Doris’s case it was a 3 dimensional heart.

00:13:58.290 --> 00:14:02.830
In our case it’s this simple sheet of material
that we can keep as a sheet.

00:14:02.830 --> 00:14:05.780
We could turn it into a hydro gel.

00:14:05.780 --> 00:14:08.210
We can turn it into a liquid.

00:14:08.210 --> 00:14:14.050
And you’re looking at muscle cells there
that were formed, in it wasn’t Sargent Smith’s

00:14:14.050 --> 00:14:19.990
but one of our other patients in a recent
study where we treated 13 patients like Sargent

00:14:19.990 --> 00:14:27.150
Smith, all of whom returned to a quality of
life where they can do the activities of daily

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:28.630
living like he did.

00:14:28.630 --> 00:14:36.570
Basically what we do is change the default-this
material has the ability to signal the body

00:14:36.570 --> 00:14:41.120
to say look instead of responding to this
injury by forming scar, what we want to do

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:46.310
is recruit the body's own cells to it and
have them assemble, as Doris was saying, the

00:14:46.310 --> 00:14:52.040
way they would normally and recreate the tissue
rather than just repair the tissue.

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:57.010
So it’s a little different approach and
like I said, none of us have the solutions

00:14:57.010 --> 00:15:00.590
to all of the problems but we all get there
a little different way.

00:15:00.590 --> 00:15:01.590
Absolutely.

00:15:01.590 --> 00:15:02.590
Unbelievable.

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:04.480
Dany, you’re using electricity.

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:06.260
Is that fair to say?

00:15:06.260 --> 00:15:07.720
To make…

00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:09.380
That sounds Frankensteinian.

00:15:09.380 --> 00:15:10.820
It’s very Frankensteinian.

00:15:10.820 --> 00:15:15.310
I love the analogy.

00:15:15.310 --> 00:15:22.750
The idea is that by studying these electrical
signals during development and seeing that

00:15:22.750 --> 00:15:30.060
they can be the signals that says, ok you
be a muscle cell, these cells start dividing,

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:34.700
those cells stop dividing, that this is one
of the ways that cells talk to each other.

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:38.440
So chemicals is one important way.

00:15:38.440 --> 00:15:40.240
But electricity is another important way.

00:15:40.240 --> 00:15:41.780
It’s just the movement of ions.

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:46.910
So everybody has heard of pH, which is just
a fancy way of saying a concentration of hydrogen

00:15:46.910 --> 00:15:48.230
ions.

00:15:48.230 --> 00:15:52.230
And hydrogen ions moving, that’s a current,
that’s electricity.

00:15:52.230 --> 00:16:00.170
And we found that there are so many places
in development of the embryo where those electrical

00:16:00.170 --> 00:16:05.920
signals seem to be…I hesitate to use the
term, but master switches.

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:11.660
They really do seem to be like you flip a
switch and then a process gets started.

00:16:11.660 --> 00:16:15.630
And very importantly, it doesn’t just get
started, it gets controlled.

00:16:15.630 --> 00:16:18.800
And it gets stopped at the right time.

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:25.010
So we say, ok will this work in regeneration
as well?

00:16:25.010 --> 00:16:33.660
And have found that yes, again in that situation
these very, very powerful signals that coordinate.

00:16:33.660 --> 00:16:37.759
They take care of everything.

00:16:37.759 --> 00:16:45.160
What you’re seeing here actually is one
of the really important stages in vertebrates,

00:16:45.160 --> 00:16:46.690
things with backbones.

00:16:46.690 --> 00:16:52.020
In regenerations of vertebrates is that you
have to have nerves there for the rest of

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:53.680
the structure to regenerate.

00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:59.110
And what you’re looking at here is a lot
of nerves that have grown in places that they

00:16:59.110 --> 00:17:00.110
don’t necessarily belong.

00:17:00.110 --> 00:17:05.309
That’s a close-up of a fin of a tadpole,
of a frog.

00:17:05.309 --> 00:17:12.240
This is work by Dr. Douglas Blackiston and
what he did to get that to happen was he simply

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:19.149
soaked the embryo, sorry, soaked the tadpole
in serotonin so the neurotransmitter.

00:17:19.149 --> 00:17:26.429
And what happens through a chemical signal
pathway is the serotonin makes the cells in

00:17:26.429 --> 00:17:34.000
that fin be a little bit more positive than
they would normally be and that attracts the

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:35.000
nerves.

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:42.679
And so a place with no nerves suddenly has
this huge invasion by all these nerves.

00:17:42.679 --> 00:17:50.799
He’s actually done some truly remarkable
work where he has a combination of transplant

00:17:50.799 --> 00:17:53.690
but then looking at what happens to the nerves.

00:17:53.690 --> 00:18:01.669
He puts a piece of tissue that would be an
eye and he puts it into the flank of another

00:18:01.669 --> 00:18:02.669
embryo.

00:18:02.669 --> 00:18:04.190
So puts it in the wrong place.

00:18:04.190 --> 00:18:08.559
And he uses an eye because that will then
send out a large nerve, the optic nerve.

00:18:08.559 --> 00:18:15.429
And sometimes that optic nerve actually finds
its way to the spinal cord and that eye can

00:18:15.429 --> 00:18:18.850
sense light, it can see.

00:18:18.850 --> 00:18:24.139
So the brain somehow knows that its visual
information even though it’s coming from

00:18:24.139 --> 00:18:25.679
all the wrong place.

00:18:25.679 --> 00:18:33.940
But again it’s this idea of being able to
control and use those electoral signals to

00:18:33.940 --> 00:18:35.269
get what we need to happen.

00:18:35.269 --> 00:18:42.499
In this case for nerves to grow and in other
cases, for entire limbs to reproduce themselves.

00:18:42.499 --> 00:18:45.600
Show us what that’s about.

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:53.210
So what you’re looking at here is the back
end of a frog and if a frog loses a limb,

00:18:53.210 --> 00:18:59.059
what you see here is the left hand side, that’s
a completely regenerated limb.

00:18:59.059 --> 00:19:05.470
That was amputated up close to the knee there
and normally if that happens, you get what

00:19:05.470 --> 00:19:10.730
is called a spike and it’s exactly what
it sounds like, it’s a spike.

00:19:10.730 --> 00:19:15.539
But this was treated, this is the work of
Dr. Ai Sun Tseng, who when she was working

00:19:15.539 --> 00:19:24.320
with Mike Levin, and she treated that wound
with a little cocktail and that cocktail did

00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:25.320
the same thing.

00:19:25.320 --> 00:19:30.640
It made the cells change their electrical
characteristics.

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:34.190
And 6 months later, this grew back.

00:19:34.190 --> 00:19:37.740
So instead of getting a spike, she got a foot.

00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:42.080
And this is a foot, you can see it’s got
the blood vessels, it’s got skin, it’s

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:47.200
got muscles, it’s got bone and very importantly
it has nerves.

00:19:47.200 --> 00:19:50.070
And it’s also the right shape.

00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:54.610
And what you’re seeing her is she’s just
poking that to show you that it’s quite

00:19:54.610 --> 00:20:00.999
responsive, it has a very well formed and
functional nervous system.

00:20:00.999 --> 00:20:08.590
So this incredibly simple message months ago
which was, change your electrical potential

00:20:08.590 --> 00:20:15.769
and this entire limb has grown back and it’s
the right shape and it stops when it’s done.

00:20:15.769 --> 00:20:16.789
Unbelievable.

00:20:16.789 --> 00:20:25.340
It’s quite extraordinary work.

00:20:25.340 --> 00:20:34.850
So give us the, Jonathan, the how to print
a heart valve for dummies, if you will.

00:20:34.850 --> 00:20:41.700
Well I think we’re all aware of this new
revolution of 3D printing and these maker

00:20:41.700 --> 00:20:44.500
communities and maker spaces.

00:20:44.500 --> 00:20:51.380
All sorts of printers that are now entering
the community with the ability to make almost

00:20:51.380 --> 00:20:52.380
anything.

00:20:52.380 --> 00:20:58.049
And I say almost because making something
that’s completely living is very different

00:20:58.049 --> 00:21:00.139
than making something out of plastic.

00:21:00.139 --> 00:21:06.270
But in reality, anyone who has ever made a
birthday cake can 3D print something that

00:21:06.270 --> 00:21:09.070
is as complex as a heart valve.

00:21:09.070 --> 00:21:13.799
I would say that the way that we need to do
it differently is because every patient has

00:21:13.799 --> 00:21:22.580
a different heart valve, or heart or whatever,
but that geometric complexity is actually

00:21:22.580 --> 00:21:28.119
extremely important for that particular success
of that particular graft for that patient.

00:21:28.119 --> 00:21:33.619
So what we have to do is work with surgeons
to get clinical imaging which you see here

00:21:33.619 --> 00:21:41.809
that defines the actual geometry needed to
extract and then replicate in a 3D printer.

00:21:41.809 --> 00:21:48.200
Now once you have that geometry, you can actually
use computer algorithms to develop a deposition

00:21:48.200 --> 00:21:53.909
or printing path and you can use a printer
like the one you see here to essentially deposit

00:21:53.909 --> 00:21:56.480
material along those defined paths.

00:21:56.480 --> 00:22:00.480
Now the challenge becomes what do you print
because it’s not going to be frosting or

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:01.530
plastic.

00:22:01.530 --> 00:22:07.509
So we actually develop in our lab these materials,
we could call them living inks or slurries

00:22:07.509 --> 00:22:15.230
and they’re actually comprised of biologically
derived components of extracellular matrix,

00:22:15.230 --> 00:22:19.960
potentially from tissues like the ones that
Doris and Stephen are talking about but also

00:22:19.960 --> 00:22:25.919
we can make them from scratch and the idea
is that you can design point by point the

00:22:25.919 --> 00:22:27.249
material you want where.

00:22:27.249 --> 00:22:32.190
And also we can embed stem cells in those
same environments and the opportunity is that

00:22:32.190 --> 00:22:37.179
now you can put the instructions you want
to give cells in each location so that in

00:22:37.179 --> 00:22:41.400
one location, they make one kind of cell and
in another location the stem cells turn into

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:45.409
a different type of cell all the while achieving
the geometric complexity

00:22:45.409 --> 00:22:47.330
So that’s what that is?

00:22:47.330 --> 00:22:54.559
Yeah, so that’s a human, it’s an aortic
heart valve, a tri leaflet heart valve that

00:22:54.559 --> 00:22:59.490
we’ve manufactured and you can see a couple
different sizes that we make here because

00:22:59.490 --> 00:23:05.740
one of the major clinical needs for this type
of technology is actually the pediatric populations.

00:23:05.740 --> 00:23:10.960
In particular for a scaffold, for a graft
like this that has the living capacity to

00:23:10.960 --> 00:23:14.720
grow and so you can’t really use a prosthetic
for that.

00:23:14.720 --> 00:23:20.249
So there’s really nothing other that this
kind of opportunity to go so that’s why

00:23:20.249 --> 00:23:23.110
we’re pushing this envelope.

00:23:23.110 --> 00:23:32.300
OK, so one of the things I think we have to
talk about is the elephant in the room, which

00:23:32.300 --> 00:23:34.950
is genetics and genomics.

00:23:34.950 --> 00:23:39.950
That’s an inside joke.

00:23:39.950 --> 00:23:45.860
There is a paradigm shift here because you
guys aren’t doing anything with genes.

00:23:45.860 --> 00:23:52.179
You’re using, you’re coming at it from
a completely different angle.

00:23:52.179 --> 00:23:58.580
Tell me about the paradigm shift and how it
happened and what it means.

00:23:58.580 --> 00:24:05.749
Well to me, genes as I said earlier if you’re
going to work with genes, you have to have

00:24:05.749 --> 00:24:10.850
the right gene and you have to have a cell
for that gene to work.

00:24:10.850 --> 00:24:15.249
Genes don’t happen in a vacuum, they only
work in a cell.

00:24:15.249 --> 00:24:19.789
So we said we can spend the rest of our lives
trying to pick the right gene, figure out

00:24:19.789 --> 00:24:26.340
the right gene or we can take cells which
are little genes factories and let nature

00:24:26.340 --> 00:24:33.909
drive which genes by providing the right environment
for those genes for those cells, whether it’s

00:24:33.909 --> 00:24:35.830
a chemical, a scaffold or whatever.

00:24:35.830 --> 00:24:39.239
Genes are the potential.

00:24:39.239 --> 00:24:44.299
Cells are the reality.

00:24:44.299 --> 00:24:45.299
OK.

00:24:45.299 --> 00:24:46.299
Stephen?

00:24:46.299 --> 00:24:47.299
I pretty much agree with Doris.

00:24:47.299 --> 00:24:53.309
I think I’m a little more positive on the
future of using the human genome and our understanding

00:24:53.309 --> 00:24:57.940
of molecular biology for personalized medicine
because each of us has our own unique set

00:24:57.940 --> 00:24:59.009
of genes.

00:24:59.009 --> 00:25:04.559
I think that the more we understand about
your set versus my set, you know one particular

00:25:04.559 --> 00:25:07.080
therapy is going to work better for you than
for me.

00:25:07.080 --> 00:25:13.360
I think that’s where we’re really going
to use that particular approach to regenerative

00:25:13.360 --> 00:25:14.520
medicine.

00:25:14.520 --> 00:25:20.529
One of the things that I find most interesting
about what we do or what makes my job so much

00:25:20.529 --> 00:25:26.960
fun every day is when we put an extracellular
matrix either in a petri dish or in a patient

00:25:26.960 --> 00:25:29.129
why do certain cells get attracted.

00:25:29.129 --> 00:25:35.389
What tells-what signals in that matrix, tell
that stem cell that you should now differentiate

00:25:35.389 --> 00:25:38.980
into a bone or cartilage or whatever.

00:25:38.980 --> 00:25:43.110
And it gets down to the molecular signals
that are there.

00:25:43.110 --> 00:25:45.870
So what are those signaling pathways?

00:25:45.870 --> 00:25:56.740
Here’s a real life example, without getting
into too much detail, we now understand how…why

00:25:56.740 --> 00:26:01.860
can you take a pig extracellular matrix, put
it into a human and not have problems, right?

00:26:01.860 --> 00:26:03.909
Why doesn’t it get rejected?

00:26:03.909 --> 00:26:05.789
So we’re very interested in that question.

00:26:05.789 --> 00:26:11.710
One of the reason is because the molecular
makeup of the proteins and the other molecules

00:26:11.710 --> 00:26:17.700
that form my extracellular matrix and yours
and a pig’s and a mouse and probably a dinosaur

00:26:17.700 --> 00:26:25.179
is so similar that the recipient says, ok
you might not be me, but you’re not so bad.

00:26:25.179 --> 00:26:32.799
I think I understand, you know so we don’t
reject it and actually it breaks it down and

00:26:32.799 --> 00:26:35.049
it’s loaded with these signaling molecules.

00:26:35.049 --> 00:26:39.220
And so one of the things we found out about
in a recent study because we were interested

00:26:39.220 --> 00:26:44.890
in the immune system, was what tells the immune
system to accept and not reject?

00:26:44.890 --> 00:26:52.409
Well the intracellular pathway involves the
same pathway that is inhibited by non-steroidal

00:26:52.409 --> 00:26:57.200
anti-inflammatory NSAIDs, like Advil or Naproxen,
things like this.

00:26:57.200 --> 00:27:03.899
So what we learned is if we put the patient
on these non-steroidal drugs after surgery

00:27:03.899 --> 00:27:08.100
for pain, you’re actually blocking a pathway
that’s important.

00:27:08.100 --> 00:27:10.000
So from that standpoint it’s really…

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:11.640
It’s all inflammation, Steve.

00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:12.700
It is.

00:27:12.700 --> 00:27:14.399
It is.

00:27:14.399 --> 00:27:21.389
I’m even sorry that word exists because
it implies a negative connotation and without

00:27:21.389 --> 00:27:24.240
inflammation, you know none of us would be
here.

00:27:24.240 --> 00:27:27.739
Inflammation is nature’s cue to say, I’ve
got an injury.

00:27:27.739 --> 00:27:28.929
Send me cells.

00:27:28.929 --> 00:27:35.899
I’m going to finish with one more comment
because Jonathan’s an engineer, Dany’s

00:27:35.899 --> 00:27:45.269
a molecular biologist, I’m a physician and
Doris, Doris is everything but none of us

00:27:45.269 --> 00:27:46.649
have the answers.

00:27:46.649 --> 00:27:52.769
An interdisciplinary approach to these problems,
of using the molecular biologist and the engineering

00:27:52.769 --> 00:27:55.739
and such is where the greatest advances are
going to be made.

00:27:55.739 --> 00:28:02.850
You know one of our problems is that we’re
so-our method of educating is ancient and

00:28:02.850 --> 00:28:06.369
it hasn’t changed much.

00:28:06.369 --> 00:28:12.169
But we are recognizing that interdisciplinary
approaches to problems yield the biggest solutions.

00:28:12.169 --> 00:28:19.610
Before we get too far away from the genome
and genetics is one reason we don’t study

00:28:19.610 --> 00:28:21.700
it is because there are lots of people studying
it.

00:28:21.700 --> 00:28:24.590
They’re doing tremendous, wonderful work.

00:28:24.590 --> 00:28:27.019
And that’s not what we’re interested in.

00:28:27.019 --> 00:28:32.350
But that’s being done, really beautifully
done in labs all over the world.

00:28:32.350 --> 00:28:37.429
But the other part of that is, it hasn’t
worked yet.

00:28:37.429 --> 00:28:41.889
Nobody’s taken that stem cell and turned
it into a heart.

00:28:41.889 --> 00:28:48.970
Nobody has said these genes are the things
that give you this shape.

00:28:48.970 --> 00:28:50.090
Got it.

00:28:50.090 --> 00:28:55.590
And in fact, there are these people now who
work on these things called gene regulatory

00:28:55.590 --> 00:29:00.909
networks and these are genes that turn each
other on and off and trying to figure it out.

00:29:00.909 --> 00:29:07.200
What is actually going on, who’s the messenger
passing from to almost like a game of telephone.

00:29:07.200 --> 00:29:11.190
And you can have the gene regulatory networks.

00:29:11.190 --> 00:29:18.600
You can think of as a map and they have you
know one map that has 50 different things

00:29:18.600 --> 00:29:23.059
in it, gives you a tube that’s a blood vessel.

00:29:23.059 --> 00:29:26.900
But if you have another tube that’s for
something different, the gene regulatory network

00:29:26.900 --> 00:29:28.109
could be completely different.

00:29:28.109 --> 00:29:30.309
There’s nothing in there yet.

00:29:30.309 --> 00:29:35.230
We don’t know yet how to look at that and
see where a shape actually comes from.

00:29:35.230 --> 00:29:43.289
And that’s because it doesn’t, we haven’t
yet figured how to bring in geometry and forces

00:29:43.289 --> 00:29:46.629
and directing forces and resisting them and
generating them.

00:29:46.629 --> 00:29:48.840
And it just simply hasn’t worked yet.

00:29:48.840 --> 00:29:54.450
Don’t you think nature has developed a lot
of different ways though to create the same

00:29:54.450 --> 00:29:55.450
things?

00:29:55.450 --> 00:29:56.450
Absolutely.

00:29:56.450 --> 00:30:02.269
If you look at it, we have tubes in our blood
vessels, we have tubes in our lungs, we have

00:30:02.269 --> 00:30:04.389
tubes in our kidneys, in our bladder.

00:30:04.389 --> 00:30:05.840
Tubes are great.

00:30:05.840 --> 00:30:07.289
We are tubes.

00:30:07.289 --> 00:30:08.289
Everything.

00:30:08.289 --> 00:30:09.289
Right.

00:30:09.289 --> 00:30:11.159
We’re a little more than that.

00:30:11.159 --> 00:30:18.260
So the body has created, there are a number
of to create every single thing that exists

00:30:18.260 --> 00:30:23.279
so it’s not one gene one endpoint.

00:30:23.279 --> 00:30:31.679
And I think what we’re doing, at least my
view of it is we’re bypassing a lot of that

00:30:31.679 --> 00:30:34.350
and we’re saying we’ve got the shape.

00:30:34.350 --> 00:30:39.269
We’re letting nature, we’re taking advantage
of what nature’s already built with those

00:30:39.269 --> 00:30:47.649
cues already in it and letting that drive
what we decide to do going forward.

00:30:47.649 --> 00:30:53.249
So we can spend the next 20 years figuring
out all those networks or we can use what

00:30:53.249 --> 00:30:55.779
nature has built and go forward.

00:30:55.779 --> 00:30:57.619
Stephen do you want to add to that?

00:30:57.619 --> 00:31:02.340
Well I was just going to, I was trying to
figure out how to get this comment in.

00:31:02.340 --> 00:31:11.190
but maybe- developmental biology, if we don’t
understand normal development, how do we expect

00:31:11.190 --> 00:31:16.710
to develop effective strategies for recreating
normal functional tissues?

00:31:16.710 --> 00:31:18.869
So we have a couple of regenerative organs
in our body.

00:31:18.869 --> 00:31:25.370
The liver is constantly regenerating by a
mechanism that’s different than the axolotl,

00:31:25.370 --> 00:31:26.370
but it’s a regenerative organ.

00:31:26.370 --> 00:31:29.749
Our bone marrow regenerates all our blood
cells every day.

00:31:29.749 --> 00:31:36.129
Outer layers of our skin and one or two other
tissues but the rest, through evolution, the

00:31:36.129 --> 00:31:42.809
rest of our tissues have changed to say we’ve
sacrificed regeneration for healing, for scar

00:31:42.809 --> 00:31:44.480
tissue formation.

00:31:44.480 --> 00:31:49.369
It’s really interesting I think in developmental
biology if you look at-the human fetus in

00:31:49.369 --> 00:31:55.130
the first trimester can regenerate all sorts
of organs and somewhere around 16 to 18 weeks

00:31:55.130 --> 00:31:59.940
of gestation, right when our immune system
kicks in, we develop this ability to fight

00:31:59.940 --> 00:32:04.260
off infection and respond to injury, we lose
the ability to regenerate.

00:32:04.260 --> 00:32:06.889
So which genes are being turned on and which
genes are being turned off?

00:32:06.889 --> 00:32:11.850
And I think if we would just work together
a little more, Dany, we’d get the

00:32:11.850 --> 00:32:13.940
But that’s an important point.

00:32:13.940 --> 00:32:20.740
That there’s a road map there for regeneration
that could be understood better.

00:32:20.740 --> 00:32:26.119
But you know, you can think about that even
after we’re born, if you look at a two year

00:32:26.119 --> 00:32:32.409
old and they fall down and scrape their knee,
it’s going to get red, inflammation is going

00:32:32.409 --> 00:32:39.350
to happen and that’s nature’s cue to send
me more cells and they heal that.

00:32:39.350 --> 00:32:42.610
But they probably don’t have a scar for
the rest of their life.

00:32:42.610 --> 00:32:46.019
Two year old gets a cold, it goes away in
3 days.

00:32:46.019 --> 00:32:51.350
They’ve got cells, they’ve got potent
healthy cells that we don’t have.

00:32:51.350 --> 00:32:56.990
When you’re 52, 62 you fall down and scrape
your knee, the cues aren’t the same.

00:32:56.990 --> 00:33:01.619
There’s a wonderful story about that actually
that up to the age of 7 and depending on who

00:33:01.619 --> 00:33:06.499
tells you the story, it’s 5, sometimes it’s
10, that when you’re a kid, you can actually

00:33:06.499 --> 00:33:08.989
lose the tip of your finger.

00:33:08.989 --> 00:33:16.729
Generally the standard procedure is to take
the skin and wrap it over to prevent infection.

00:33:16.729 --> 00:33:21.440
But if you don’t do that there, is a current,
called the healing current and it’s just

00:33:21.440 --> 00:33:27.350
positive charges leaving the tip of the cell,
tip of the finger and the finger will regenerate.

00:33:27.350 --> 00:33:32.720
And it will regenerate the skin and the bone
and the muscle and the nail and we lose that

00:33:32.720 --> 00:33:40.539
ability somewhere 7, 10 something like that
but what that tells us and the liver and all

00:33:40.539 --> 00:33:43.940
these things tells us that our cells do know
how to do this.

00:33:43.940 --> 00:33:47.899
Heart cells can do that for two weeks after
birth.

00:33:47.899 --> 00:33:49.059
For 2 weeks.

00:33:49.059 --> 00:33:53.049
So all of our cells seem to know, it’s in
there somewhere.

00:33:53.049 --> 00:33:57.970
And whether we need genetics to trigger it,
if we can help them along.

00:33:57.970 --> 00:34:02.679
I know that some people now, correct me if
I’m wrong, one of the treatments for losing

00:34:02.679 --> 00:34:10.730
a fingertip is to pack the wound with pig
bladder to provide the scaffold.

00:34:10.730 --> 00:34:17.869
There’s learning how it’s done and learning
how we might manipulate it to trigger it.

00:34:17.869 --> 00:34:23.250
Now whether that’s exactly how it was done
when the embryo was developing and turning

00:34:23.250 --> 00:34:31.169
into a fetus or whether we do it by tricking
it, turning on the wrong gene but it has the

00:34:31.169 --> 00:34:36.960
right effect, that’s where it turns into
medicine, that’s when you go to translation

00:34:36.960 --> 00:34:43.720
and how do we- we don’t care how it was
done we care how to make it happen again.

00:34:43.720 --> 00:34:53.099
Let’s talk about what are some of the actual
term and near term benefits of what we’re

00:34:53.099 --> 00:34:54.940
talking about tonight.

00:34:54.940 --> 00:35:00.430
Who, where do we see things, if not right
now, very soon?

00:35:00.430 --> 00:35:01.430
Jonathan.

00:35:01.430 --> 00:35:05.869
Well I think the things that are right now
are the kind of thing that you saw in the

00:35:05.869 --> 00:35:13.700
movie here, the kinds of things that Steve
is doing with being able to augment the humans’

00:35:13.700 --> 00:35:21.599
own capacity to heal, whether it’s regenerating
the tip of your finger or healing a certain

00:35:21.599 --> 00:35:25.960
injury, better like a diabetic ulcer or something
like that.

00:35:25.960 --> 00:35:32.281
I think in the more near term, we’re going
to see and there are a lot of trials that

00:35:32.281 --> 00:35:40.579
are going on right now in this space for tissues
that perform an important role, say an occupational

00:35:40.579 --> 00:35:44.030
role, but they’re not essential for life.

00:35:44.030 --> 00:35:49.250
Like for example, vertebral disk or a knee
meniscus, or something like this.

00:35:49.250 --> 00:35:56.369
This is the are that I think will be the first
place where we’ll start to see some of these

00:35:56.369 --> 00:36:00.210
tissue engineering strategies, bio-fabrication
come into play.

00:36:00.210 --> 00:36:01.950
Dany, what about?

00:36:01.950 --> 00:36:07.060
Right now, what do you see using your electrodes?

00:36:07.060 --> 00:36:12.190
Right now we see amazing possibilities.

00:36:12.190 --> 00:36:24.599
I think we’re still far from it but in some
ways we may be able to skip more quickly that

00:36:24.599 --> 00:36:32.240
other types of therapies from making a frog
do it to making a human do it.

00:36:32.240 --> 00:36:38.940
The reason for that is there are so many drugs
that are already available.

00:36:38.940 --> 00:36:41.700
Some of them over the counter.

00:36:41.700 --> 00:36:50.450
That what these drugs target are these proteins
that make electrical things happen.

00:36:50.450 --> 00:36:57.020
So if you’ve ever taken a proton pump inhibitor
for you know stomach acid, the proton pump

00:36:57.020 --> 00:37:03.390
is a protein and it moves hydrogen ions which
are from inside the cells to outside the cells.

00:37:03.390 --> 00:37:10.971
And that, you know just, when I was working
with tadpole tail regenerations, we found

00:37:10.971 --> 00:37:17.530
that all you had to do to get the entire tail
to regenerate was give it a message.

00:37:17.530 --> 00:37:22.960
And we did that in a number of different ways,
but give it the message, pump protons and

00:37:22.960 --> 00:37:25.089
we got the entire tail.

00:37:25.089 --> 00:37:31.630
So we have drugs that you can take now that
pump protons.

00:37:31.630 --> 00:37:36.770
Or they stop your proton pump from pumping,
they’re inhibitors.

00:37:36.770 --> 00:37:44.710
I told the story about serotonin, having this
tremendous effect via its effect on the electrical

00:37:44.710 --> 00:37:46.309
status of the cells.

00:37:46.309 --> 00:37:50.160
Well, Prozac can have the same kind of effect.

00:37:50.160 --> 00:37:55.630
We have all these drugs and their targets
are these electrical signals.

00:37:55.630 --> 00:38:01.630
So in some ways it could be very rapid because
we already have the medications, but we do

00:38:01.630 --> 00:38:08.270
have a long way to go in terms of understanding
for any given organ that you lose.

00:38:08.270 --> 00:38:12.030
You have heart damage, we don’t know, oh
well yeah if you take Prilosec your heart

00:38:12.030 --> 00:38:13.030
will grow back.

00:38:13.030 --> 00:38:14.470
We don’t know that yet.

00:38:14.470 --> 00:38:18.500
But we’re getting there.

00:38:18.500 --> 00:38:20.599
It won’t.

00:38:20.599 --> 00:38:27.660
So it's possible that once we really decipher
that language , and we can speak it, we can

00:38:27.660 --> 00:38:29.920
go in there and start fixing the grammar.

00:38:29.920 --> 00:38:34.030
And it would be potentially as easy as taking
a pill.

00:38:34.030 --> 00:38:41.420
Doris, I’ll get to you last but your day
to day is app…using these applications,

00:38:41.420 --> 00:38:45.619
is that correct?

00:38:45.619 --> 00:38:47.079
Tell us how you do that.

00:38:47.079 --> 00:38:52.590
We’ve got between 8 &amp; 10 million patients
have been treated with AN extracellular matrix

00:38:52.590 --> 00:38:58.590
scaffold, but most of them are for rather
ordinary, everyday things.

00:38:58.590 --> 00:39:02.599
And there’s good reason for that, like hernia
repair.

00:39:02.599 --> 00:39:07.130
Instead of using a synthetic mesh made out
of polypropylene you would make it out of

00:39:07.130 --> 00:39:10.150
extracellular matrix and these are used for
that now.

00:39:10.150 --> 00:39:16.079
You know 20 years ago they didn’t exist,
those sorts of scaffold materials didn’t

00:39:16.079 --> 00:39:17.079
exist.

00:39:17.079 --> 00:39:19.609
Same thing, it’s used for the lining of
the brain to replace that.

00:39:19.609 --> 00:39:23.800
So there’s lots of those everyday things
and the reason those things got to the clinic

00:39:23.800 --> 00:39:31.950
so quickly is because they are regulated as
a device by the FDA.

00:39:31.950 --> 00:39:37.049
They were promoted in a non-regenerative medicine
approach, rather than simply say, hey look

00:39:37.049 --> 00:39:39.599
we just got a different type of mesh material
for you.

00:39:39.599 --> 00:39:42.819
So it gets out there and now we have all of
these patients treated.

00:39:42.819 --> 00:39:47.250
One more thing cause it’s an aspect of it
that isn’t scientific but you might not

00:39:47.250 --> 00:39:51.280
think of in terms of why you don’t see more
clinical translations.

00:39:51.280 --> 00:39:56.920
When you have a regenerative medicine approach
that is significantly different than the standard

00:39:56.920 --> 00:40:02.530
of care, and even though you’re absolutely
convinced it works, you can point to all of

00:40:02.530 --> 00:40:06.150
these studies and you go and you have a patient
that walks through the door and you want to

00:40:06.150 --> 00:40:11.230
use that, and you go ahead and use it, your
colleagues are looking at you saying, what

00:40:11.230 --> 00:40:12.230
are you doing?

00:40:12.230 --> 00:40:15.850
Don’t you know, we’ve been taught how
to treat these patients.

00:40:15.850 --> 00:40:19.950
Aren’t you…so you’ve got peer pressure
to say don’t do it.

00:40:19.950 --> 00:40:23.470
And then you got how are you going to get
it reimbursed?

00:40:23.470 --> 00:40:28.829
Because you’ve got a solution here but no
way to pay for that right now and until you

00:40:28.829 --> 00:40:32.530
prove it 10 years later.

00:40:32.530 --> 00:40:37.619
And on top of that you’ve got lawyers standing
out there waiting to sue you the first time

00:40:37.619 --> 00:40:38.799
anything goes wrong.

00:40:38.799 --> 00:40:40.869
So what are the incentives to do these?

00:40:40.869 --> 00:40:44.039
You’ve got your colleagues saying no, you’re
not going to get paid for it, you’re at

00:40:44.039 --> 00:40:47.059
risk of getting sued and yet your patient
said please.

00:40:47.059 --> 00:40:54.540
Really, that’s as big a barrier as a scientific
barrier for translating regenerative medicine.

00:40:54.540 --> 00:40:58.220
And that’s the heartache of being an innovator,
right?

00:40:58.220 --> 00:41:04.510
Being someone who is outside the box, not
in the mainstream, which probably I think

00:41:04.510 --> 00:41:10.550
all of you, maybe Jonathan a little less than
others, have faced a little ostracism.

00:41:10.550 --> 00:41:11.780
Is that fair to say?

00:41:11.780 --> 00:41:14.809
Oh yeah, oh yeah.

00:41:14.809 --> 00:41:15.809
Frankenstein.

00:41:15.809 --> 00:41:18.589
Yes, of course, right.

00:41:18.589 --> 00:41:20.579
What about you Doris?

00:41:20.579 --> 00:41:24.090
And then we’ll talk about money because
we promised we were going to discuss that.

00:41:24.090 --> 00:41:30.799
The thing about regenerative medicine is it’s
unlike any other treatment in that when you

00:41:30.799 --> 00:41:35.882
give it, you know, you give a drug, it’s
going to wear off.

00:41:35.882 --> 00:41:39.859
You give a drug it may have an effect you
know and one you don’t.

00:41:39.859 --> 00:41:45.940
Apparently proton pump inhibitors grow salamander
tails inside you, I don’t want that.

00:41:45.940 --> 00:41:52.119
But the point is with regenerative medicine
strategies, they’re forever.

00:41:52.119 --> 00:41:56.680
So when you give them, you have to know they
work, you have to know that they’re going

00:41:56.680 --> 00:42:06.150
to hang around for the long term and be safe,
and you have to know that they’re actually

00:42:06.150 --> 00:42:14.930
going to be flexible and change as the individual
changes and faces other issues.

00:42:14.930 --> 00:42:21.799
So the burden is higher for these than for
any other therapy that exists.

00:42:21.799 --> 00:42:25.760
At the same time, the potential is huge.

00:42:25.760 --> 00:42:32.210
Right now, we may not be able to build a heart
in a lab, but I predict within the next 2-3

00:42:32.210 --> 00:42:38.140
years we’ll see a liver be built and transplanted
into a patient.

00:42:38.140 --> 00:42:41.670
We will see, I guarantee you we’ll see a
liver.

00:42:41.670 --> 00:42:46.720
Beyond that, pancreases are being built.

00:42:46.720 --> 00:42:49.050
Lungs.

00:42:49.050 --> 00:42:51.089
I was going to say, lungs are being built.

00:42:51.089 --> 00:42:54.930
We’re going to see these things happen.

00:42:54.930 --> 00:43:02.420
But even today we can build a cardiac patch,
we can build new blood vessels, we can build

00:43:02.420 --> 00:43:05.740
a lot of tissues that are simple tubes.

00:43:05.740 --> 00:43:09.930
Those tubes we were talking about earlier.

00:43:09.930 --> 00:43:12.339
Bladder.

00:43:12.339 --> 00:43:14.600
New cosmetic ears.

00:43:14.600 --> 00:43:20.520
There are a number of strategies that already
exist.

00:43:20.520 --> 00:43:25.579
And for, I would go so far as to say we’re
not building organs and tissues, we’re building

00:43:25.579 --> 00:43:26.579
hope.

00:43:26.579 --> 00:43:35.730
We’re building the future and the potential
for us to actually do what we’re talking

00:43:35.730 --> 00:43:39.109
about out here, forever young.

00:43:39.109 --> 00:43:45.520
We’re building the potential to keep people
healthy longer and able to function at a level

00:43:45.520 --> 00:43:47.569
that they haven’t been able to before.

00:43:47.569 --> 00:43:51.920
I think something thing to keep in mind too
is people say well, what are we going to do

00:43:51.920 --> 00:43:55.680
if we can solve all of these problems and
live to be 120.

00:43:55.680 --> 00:44:01.151
I don’t want to live to 120 and you don’t
want to hear me talking about these same things

00:44:01.151 --> 00:44:04.089
for how many x number of years.

00:44:04.089 --> 00:44:08.970
But you want to be healthy.

00:44:08.970 --> 00:44:09.980
Quality not quantity.

00:44:09.980 --> 00:44:10.980
That’s right.

00:44:10.980 --> 00:44:16.170
We’d all like to live to be 80-85 and functional.

00:44:16.170 --> 00:44:20.320
Have all of our faculties up until a couple
days before we go, right?

00:44:20.320 --> 00:44:22.549
Then you’re gone.

00:44:22.549 --> 00:44:24.500
That’s fine.

00:44:24.500 --> 00:44:25.790
They can all go at once.

00:44:25.790 --> 00:44:28.700
Everything, not a single usable part left.

00:44:28.700 --> 00:44:32.130
But you’re healthy until a week ahead, so
it’s quality not quantity.

00:44:32.130 --> 00:44:35.970
I don’t think we want to live forever.

00:44:35.970 --> 00:44:38.720
So let’s just make a couple predictions
here.

00:44:38.720 --> 00:44:40.480
It’s 2025.

00:44:40.480 --> 00:44:42.990
What’s out there?

00:44:42.990 --> 00:44:43.990
Body parts?

00:44:43.990 --> 00:44:48.980
Off the shelf heart valves?

00:44:48.980 --> 00:44:50.260
Tell me what the future is, Doris.

00:44:50.260 --> 00:44:52.039
What are we going to see?

00:44:52.039 --> 00:45:01.250
I think by 2025, if man is still alive, wasn’t
that a song?

00:45:01.250 --> 00:45:10.170
I really think that we’re going to begin
to see off the shelf organs and tissues for-

00:45:10.170 --> 00:45:17.619
we’re going to solve the organ transplant
problem, at least the liver and kidney and

00:45:17.619 --> 00:45:19.829
lung transplant problem.

00:45:19.829 --> 00:45:27.240
I also believe that we’re going to change
our endogenous repair processes so that the

00:45:27.240 --> 00:45:31.849
future is not ever getting to the point that
you need that organ.

00:45:31.849 --> 00:45:39.900
We’re going to learn how to use these cells
earlier, use stem cells that we all have in

00:45:39.900 --> 00:45:48.369
every organ and tissue and really maximize
our endogenous capacity for repair to keep

00:45:48.369 --> 00:45:51.430
us from having to have long term chronic disease.

00:45:51.430 --> 00:45:54.180
And then I do think we will be printing.

00:45:54.180 --> 00:45:59.799
I think we will be, you know, put the 4 of
us in a room and don’t let us out for 6

00:45:59.799 --> 00:46:05.059
months and Steve and Dany will get those limbs
built.

00:46:05.059 --> 00:46:12.869
I was going to say that as far as the bio-electrical
control, 8 years is not a long time to figure

00:46:12.869 --> 00:46:18.150
it out but I think that the combination…once
the electrical signals, we have a better sense

00:46:18.150 --> 00:46:26.220
of how they work, they will become a very
important part of the bioreactor into which

00:46:26.220 --> 00:46:32.430
the personalized printed organs are going
to go or the hearts, that that is going to

00:46:32.430 --> 00:46:38.119
be the first step of bringing that into it
is bringing these things together.

00:46:38.119 --> 00:46:43.000
Thinking about this culture that we’re talking
about in 8 years I mean I would love it if

00:46:43.000 --> 00:46:47.390
we could see engineers becoming an integral
part of the health care engine.

00:46:47.390 --> 00:46:51.860
So the way I see this though is sort of like
what you were saying if we have the printer

00:46:51.860 --> 00:46:58.760
in the OR or you’re thinking about using
clinical imaging to create data to inform

00:46:58.760 --> 00:47:01.890
surgical approach, should I go this way or
this way.

00:47:01.890 --> 00:47:05.230
Instead of opening up the patient and saying
well gosh but what should I do now?

00:47:05.230 --> 00:47:10.529
You actually have the information ahead of
time, you may actually have a model of it

00:47:10.529 --> 00:47:12.510
or a hologram you can stare at.

00:47:12.510 --> 00:47:16.420
I mean we know about robotic surgery like
that’s a big device.

00:47:16.420 --> 00:47:22.380
There’s a whole lot of ways to sort of democratize
this approach where every hospital has the

00:47:22.380 --> 00:47:29.859
capacity to integrate with well-trained people
that can help the doctor in their approach.

00:47:29.859 --> 00:47:31.720
Perhaps help them provide a better graft.

00:47:31.720 --> 00:47:36.950
I mean, so many of the medical revolutions
that have happened that we know about in prosthetics

00:47:36.950 --> 00:47:42.130
industry, whatever, was doctors reaching for
something around the OR because they got this

00:47:42.130 --> 00:47:44.220
thing going on with his patient.

00:47:44.220 --> 00:47:45.710
And that turned into a medical device.

00:47:45.710 --> 00:47:51.190
Well if you have an engineer that’s actually
involved in that process, you could maybe

00:47:51.190 --> 00:47:56.720
have a lot more transformative components,
but that also comes down to in that culture

00:47:56.720 --> 00:48:00.290
thinking about what Steve said, reimbursement.

00:48:00.290 --> 00:48:03.589
How does that piece get paid for?

00:48:03.589 --> 00:48:09.250
I think that this is a big component to thinking
about the overall federal strategy of supporting

00:48:09.250 --> 00:48:10.250
research.

00:48:10.250 --> 00:48:14.599
When you try to translate this research maybe
there’s a way of thinking about how citizens

00:48:14.599 --> 00:48:19.260
can get in on this new technology, but how
do we share this risk?

00:48:19.260 --> 00:48:25.250
How do we share this knowledge space in a
way that isn’t a pendulum swinging everybody

00:48:25.250 --> 00:48:28.680
getting the latest thing and everybody suing.

00:48:28.680 --> 00:48:32.859
There’s potential here to put more pieces
on the table.

00:48:32.859 --> 00:48:37.940
What we don’t talk about though, that is
a dirty little secret in science is that we’re

00:48:37.940 --> 00:48:42.619
all taught that if we don’t bring in our
own grants and our own money, then we’re

00:48:42.619 --> 00:48:43.619
not successful.

00:48:43.619 --> 00:48:49.069
That if I work with you and I help you bring
in grants, that doesn’t count for me.

00:48:49.069 --> 00:48:57.750
So part of what has to change is the recognition
that science is a team sport.

00:48:57.750 --> 00:49:05.349
And institutions have to value that team mentality
or it’s never going to happen.

00:49:05.349 --> 00:49:11.130
One of the things we talked about off line
before we began this evening is funding.

00:49:11.130 --> 00:49:16.690
And this for scientists and biomedical scientists
and bio-engineers like yourselves, this is

00:49:16.690 --> 00:49:17.960
a very big issue.

00:49:17.960 --> 00:49:20.519
Tell me what some of your concerns are.

00:49:20.519 --> 00:49:21.550
Doris, do you want to…

00:49:21.550 --> 00:49:34.410
Well, I mean science funding has gone down
over the last 2 decades and about 5 years

00:49:34.410 --> 00:49:38.730
ago, it was at the lowest it had ever been
in my lifetime.

00:49:38.730 --> 00:49:46.940
Building one heart, one human sized heart
cost me about $200,000.

00:49:46.940 --> 00:49:53.990
And until we get it reproducible and simple,
it’s going to keep costing $200,000.

00:49:53.990 --> 00:50:00.369
An average NIH grant is $250,000 a year.

00:50:00.369 --> 00:50:02.049
That’s one heart a year.

00:50:02.049 --> 00:50:04.730
That’s not going to get us there very fast.

00:50:04.730 --> 00:50:10.650
If there’s one thing that you could say
or do what would it be to ensure that funding

00:50:10.650 --> 00:50:12.640
continues?

00:50:12.640 --> 00:50:14.280
Science isn’t technology.

00:50:14.280 --> 00:50:20.130
We don’t say I’m going to go in and discover
that electrical signals can trigger regeneration.

00:50:20.130 --> 00:50:26.019
You go in and- so that movie, that electrical
face movie took me 4 years.

00:50:26.019 --> 00:50:28.750
It was an accident.

00:50:28.750 --> 00:50:36.450
The microscope where I made the movie is a
$250,000 microscope.

00:50:36.450 --> 00:50:39.200
I didn’t go in one day and say I’m going
to make this movie.

00:50:39.200 --> 00:50:40.500
It’s not engineering.

00:50:40.500 --> 00:50:43.589
It’s not problem solving.

00:50:43.589 --> 00:50:51.990
And you can put in a grant “on this date
I will have the crucial insight.”

00:50:51.990 --> 00:50:58.039
But there are some private foundations now
that are doing things in a different way and

00:50:58.039 --> 00:51:03.460
I want to give a shout out to Templeton which
has just awarded a fellowship to one of Mike

00:51:03.460 --> 00:51:08.619
Levin’s post docs to continue this frog
leg regeneration study.

00:51:08.619 --> 00:51:14.829
And to the Paul G. Allen Family Foundation
which is trying a different approach, which

00:51:14.829 --> 00:51:21.420
is to pick out a person and in this case Mike
Levin was one of those people and bet on their

00:51:21.420 --> 00:51:23.750
creativity and their productivity.

00:51:23.750 --> 00:51:29.619
And basically say, here is all of the money
you need so you don’t need to spend 60-80%

00:51:29.619 --> 00:51:34.500
of your time writing grants instead of doing
experiments.

00:51:34.500 --> 00:51:37.240
Take this and see what you can come up with.

00:51:37.240 --> 00:51:41.570
And I think that’s a very important and
useful approach.

00:51:41.570 --> 00:51:45.460
OK, we’re going to take few questions from
the audience.

00:51:45.460 --> 00:51:50.109
Charlotte age 12 has I think a really great
question.

00:51:50.109 --> 00:51:56.769
Could we improve upon nature making larger
muscles or a stronger ACL and could you do

00:51:56.769 --> 00:51:59.539
this for an extinct species?

00:51:59.539 --> 00:52:02.390
Oh man.

00:52:02.390 --> 00:52:03.390
I don’t think.

00:52:03.390 --> 00:52:04.390
Good question Charlotte.

00:52:04.390 --> 00:52:06.809
Yes, it was a good question.

00:52:06.809 --> 00:52:09.950
We get asked that a lot because of the work
we’re doing with the muscles and stuff but

00:52:09.950 --> 00:52:13.200
I don’t think we can ever improve upon Mother
Nature.

00:52:13.200 --> 00:52:17.569
We don’t even understand Mother Nature.

00:52:17.569 --> 00:52:23.299
But what we can do is hopefully harness all
of the some of the signals that she’s got

00:52:23.299 --> 00:52:27.170
to take advantage of what Mother Nature does
so well.

00:52:27.170 --> 00:52:33.660
So, to make a super human, no I don’t think
we want to do that first of all but I don’t

00:52:33.660 --> 00:52:34.670
think we can do it.

00:52:34.670 --> 00:52:37.240
Like in the example with the salamander.

00:52:37.240 --> 00:52:42.069
Why does it stop growing when it gets to here,
you know, every time.

00:52:42.069 --> 00:52:48.619
When we would actually try some of these experiments
where we would we knew we could grow new muscle,

00:52:48.619 --> 00:52:51.310
let’s do it again let’s do more.

00:52:51.310 --> 00:52:56.340
Temporarily it will grow more but then when
the body doesn’t need it, it just regresses

00:52:56.340 --> 00:52:58.200
back to normal.

00:52:58.200 --> 00:53:00.520
The body, it’s just so smart.

00:53:00.520 --> 00:53:04.289
It just makes me realize how little we understand
about it.

00:53:04.289 --> 00:53:10.270
I wrote a perspective piece a couple of months
ago called “Too Much Technology, Not Enough

00:53:10.270 --> 00:53:11.270
Biology.”

00:53:11.270 --> 00:53:14.800
So the technology and the printing and such
is fantastic but if we don’t know what we

00:53:14.800 --> 00:53:17.849
want to print, we can’t do it.

00:53:17.849 --> 00:53:21.150
That was why my no came out so quickly.

00:53:21.150 --> 00:53:22.510
But that was a great question.

00:53:22.510 --> 00:53:31.079
But the extinct specie piece, we can actually
take DNA and we can actually begin to create

00:53:31.079 --> 00:53:32.990
tissues from extinct species.

00:53:32.990 --> 00:53:37.619
I’m not sure you know, it’s like we said
earlier everything has one effect you know

00:53:37.619 --> 00:53:38.650
and one you don’t.

00:53:38.650 --> 00:53:41.612
I’m not sure we want to yet, but we could.

00:53:41.612 --> 00:53:44.280
If I could build on that.

00:53:44.280 --> 00:53:51.410
The idea that there is an optimal muscle is
I think over simplifying.

00:53:51.410 --> 00:53:58.150
So one of the things that evolution does,
evolution has to balance competing needs.

00:53:58.150 --> 00:54:07.140
So and for reasons we don’t know yet, it
may be that if a muscle gets too big, your

00:54:07.140 --> 00:54:08.910
toes fall off.

00:54:08.910 --> 00:54:12.540
But there’s a trade off that we don’t
know about.

00:54:12.540 --> 00:54:17.330
And if we try, if we pick out something that
we happen to like and say, oh I’m going

00:54:17.330 --> 00:54:23.710
to optimize that, you simply don’t know
what you’re hurting at the same time.

00:54:23.710 --> 00:54:29.840
The same muscle that you use to build a fly
wing that can do something phenomenal would

00:54:29.840 --> 00:54:32.039
not work in a human.

00:54:32.039 --> 00:54:37.940
Well, that leads great into the next question
from Erica the red head.

00:54:37.940 --> 00:54:44.320
She writes: Do you think regeneration can
be used to replace or fix damaged cells in

00:54:44.320 --> 00:54:47.680
cases like cancer or in autoimmune disorders?

00:54:47.680 --> 00:54:48.680
Absolutely .

00:54:48.680 --> 00:54:49.769
Yes, yes.

00:54:49.769 --> 00:54:50.769
Absolutely.

00:54:50.769 --> 00:54:56.680
The one thing that stem cells do better than
anything is reduce inflammation.

00:54:56.680 --> 00:54:58.480
Absolutely, no question.

00:54:58.480 --> 00:55:06.170
Well, there’s something uncomfortably close
about what you guys are doing and cancer,

00:55:06.170 --> 00:55:07.980
the concept of…

00:55:07.980 --> 00:55:09.289
So what’s a cancer cell?

00:55:09.289 --> 00:55:10.550
What’s a cancer cell?

00:55:10.550 --> 00:55:16.529
A cancer cell is a cell that can become a
lot of different things and also that reproduces.

00:55:16.529 --> 00:55:18.569
What’s a stem cell?

00:55:18.569 --> 00:55:22.170
It’s a cell that reproduces and can become
a lot of different things.

00:55:22.170 --> 00:55:30.049
The difference is a cancer cell doesn’t
know when to stop and some of us believe that

00:55:30.049 --> 00:55:34.859
some tumors are actually stem cells gone bad.

00:55:34.859 --> 00:55:41.410
So I think really understanding that interaction
is critical.

00:55:41.410 --> 00:55:45.520
None of us want to build that muscle that
is now a tumor.

00:55:45.520 --> 00:55:52.690
Has anyone seen tumorous or cancerous, unchecked
growth in the lab?

00:55:52.690 --> 00:55:54.230
Oh sure.

00:55:54.230 --> 00:55:55.230
Yes.

00:55:55.230 --> 00:55:56.539
Oh sure.

00:55:56.539 --> 00:56:02.089
How do you tell a cell is a true stem cell?

00:56:02.089 --> 00:56:06.080
Truly pluripotent can become anything stem
cell?

00:56:06.080 --> 00:56:10.119
You transplant it and watch it make a tumor.

00:56:10.119 --> 00:56:13.720
It’s not a cancerous tumor, but it’s a
tumor.

00:56:13.720 --> 00:56:15.359
It’s a teratoma.

00:56:15.359 --> 00:56:16.359
Unchecked growth.

00:56:16.359 --> 00:56:17.359
Unchecked growth.

00:56:17.359 --> 00:56:24.940
Teratomas are actually, they’re a perfect
illustration of this issue of shape being

00:56:24.940 --> 00:56:26.450
so important.

00:56:26.450 --> 00:56:34.350
So teratomas, for those of you who don’t
know, they are tumors that form often from

00:56:34.350 --> 00:56:43.880
the cells that make eggs for example and they,
the cells start to become different tissues.

00:56:43.880 --> 00:56:46.119
But they don’t know how to get into the
right shape.

00:56:46.119 --> 00:56:52.779
So what you end with is like a blob of tissue
that’s growing hair and some of its cells

00:56:52.779 --> 00:56:55.220
are producing stomach acid.

00:56:55.220 --> 00:56:58.809
They’re truly horrifying, they have teeth
sometimes.

00:56:58.809 --> 00:57:06.220
The cells are following the right path to
become a tissue and it’s called differentiation,

00:57:06.220 --> 00:57:09.829
that’s the big word for it, but they are
differentiating into different cell types

00:57:09.829 --> 00:57:12.280
just as their genes are telling them to do.

00:57:12.280 --> 00:57:16.309
But they have completely lost any information
about what shape they’re supposed to be.

00:57:16.309 --> 00:57:21.059
And so it’s a blob of tissue, cell types.

00:57:21.059 --> 00:57:28.430
So but I want to go back for a second because
cancer, regeneration, development these are

00:57:28.430 --> 00:57:32.540
all, we look at all of these things from this
perspective.

00:57:32.540 --> 00:57:37.569
Can the electrical, can looking at the electrical
signals teach us something that we haven’t

00:57:37.569 --> 00:57:41.390
learned by looking at the chemical signals
controlled by the genes.

00:57:41.390 --> 00:57:49.059
And one of the amazing, another amazing things
about electricity is that we can generate

00:57:49.059 --> 00:57:50.940
tumors genetically.

00:57:50.940 --> 00:58:00.369
So we can inject a tumor causing, a cancer
causing mutant gene and at the same time inject

00:58:00.369 --> 00:58:06.019
the gene for a protein that will allow potassium
ions in.

00:58:06.019 --> 00:58:10.869
Sorry, that will allow the potassium ions
out.

00:58:10.869 --> 00:58:14.619
And what happens is the tumor doesn’t form.

00:58:14.619 --> 00:58:22.410
So the potassium current changes the electrical
status of the cell and that tumor causing

00:58:22.410 --> 00:58:27.809
gene can’t form a tumor.

00:58:27.809 --> 00:58:33.569
The other thing you can do is you can put
in a gene, there’s this amazing thing called

00:58:33.569 --> 00:58:36.859
optogenetics, I’m not going to go into that
right now into detail, but basically these

00:58:36.859 --> 00:58:42.450
are proteins that you turn them on and off
by shining light on them.

00:58:42.450 --> 00:58:49.269
And if you make one of these tumors by injecting
this gene and you also inject the gene for

00:58:49.269 --> 00:58:54.279
one of these light activated channels, you
can wait until the tumor forms and then you

00:58:54.279 --> 00:58:57.849
turn on the light and the tumor goes away.

00:58:57.849 --> 00:59:03.849
And this in frogs, this works better that
most of our cancer drugs.

00:59:03.849 --> 00:59:08.849
So again, this connection between the electrical
signals, the way the cells are talking to

00:59:08.849 --> 00:59:18.059
each other in this context of generating a
shape, controlling a shape, making a structure.

00:59:18.059 --> 00:59:22.240
Again, I think, it’s just really powerful.

00:59:22.240 --> 00:59:23.859
Go home and thank you’re electrical signals.

00:59:23.859 --> 00:59:28.579
I think, I think she brings up a good point
though, also with another opportunity with

00:59:28.579 --> 00:59:33.930
engineering and that is to create models of
patients’ actual tumors for example and

00:59:33.930 --> 00:59:38.200
instead of testing the drugs effectiveness
on the patient, why don’t we do it on these

00:59:38.200 --> 00:59:40.220
tumors that are the patient's’ tumor.

00:59:40.220 --> 00:59:41.579
That’s already happening.

00:59:41.579 --> 00:59:48.109
Yeah, no I agree but like one of the new strategies
that is gaining traction a lot of different

00:59:48.109 --> 00:59:50.579
tumors is to actually differentiate it.

00:59:50.579 --> 00:59:53.339
So instead of having this cancerous tumor,
turn it into a pile of bone.

00:59:53.339 --> 00:59:57.819
Well if I a have a pile of bone sitting somewhere
that’s a lot better than a tumor because

00:59:57.819 --> 01:00:00.990
now it’s just sitting there as a chunk of
bone.

01:00:00.990 --> 01:00:07.049
But you could test that out in a set of engineered
tumors so you wouldn’t have to worry about

01:00:07.049 --> 01:00:08.119
trying it out on a patient.

